{"name":"Raspberry Leaf Green","hex":"#044f3b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"raspberry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The plant Rubus idaeus.","Any of many other (but not all) species in the genus Rubus."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Containing or having the flavor/flavour of raspberries.","Of a dark pinkish red."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/raspberry"},{"term":"leaf","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The usually green and flat organ that represents the most prominent feature of most vegetative plants.","A foliage leaf or any of the many and often considerably different structures it can specialise into."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To produce leaves; put forth foliage.","To divide (a vegetable) into separate leaves."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/leaf"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chlorophyll","blocks":[{"text":"Chlorophyll is any of several related photochemical catalysts and green pigments found in cyanobacteria and in the chloroplasts of algae and plants. Its name is derived from the Greek words χλωρός and φύλλον. Chlorophyll allows plants to absorb energy from light. Those pigments are involved in oxygenic photosynthesis, as opposed to bacteriochlorophylls, which are related molecules found only in bacteria and involved in anoxygenic photosynthesis."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/M%C3%A9lisse_Feuilles_FR_2013b.jpg/330px-M%C3%A9lisse_Feuilles_FR_2013b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chlorophyll"},{"title":"Red raspberry leaf","blocks":[{"text":"The red raspberry leaf, also known as garden raspberry leaf, is produced by the deciduous raspberry plant and used in folk remedies."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_raspberry_leaf"},{"title":"Raspberry","blocks":[{"text":"The raspberry is the edible fruit of several plant species in the genus Rubus of the rose family, most of which are in the subgenus Idaeobatus. The name also applies to these plants themselves. Raspberries are perennial with woody stems."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Raspberry_-_halved_%28Rubus_idaeus%29.jpg/330px-Raspberry_-_halved_%28Rubus_idaeus%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raspberry"},{"title":"Leaf","blocks":[{"text":"A leaf is a principal appendage of the stem of a vascular plant, usually borne laterally above ground and specialized for photosynthesis. Leaves are collectively called foliage, as in \"autumn foliage\", while the leaves, stem, flower, and fruit collectively form the shoot system. In most leaves, the primary photosynthetic tissue is the palisade mesophyll and is located on the upper side of the blade or lamina of the leaf, but in some species, including the mature foliage of Eucalyptus, palisade mesophyll is present on both sides and the leaves are said to be isobilateral. The leaf is an integral part of the stem system, and most leaves are flattened and have distinct upper (adaxial) and lower (abaxial) surfaces that differ in color, hairiness, the number of stomata, the amount and structure of epicuticular wax, and other features. Leaves are mostly green in color due to the presence of a compound called chlorophyll, which is essential for photosynthesis as it absorbs light energy from the Sun. A leaf with lighter-colored or white patches or edges is called a variegated leaf."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Lisc_lipy.jpg/330px-Lisc_lipy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaf"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Bryophyte_Leaf_Cells.jpg/500px-Bryophyte_Leaf_Cells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Bryophyte_Leaf_Cells.jpg/1280px-Bryophyte_Leaf_Cells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Bryophyte_Leaf_Cells.jpg/500px-Bryophyte_Leaf_Cells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABryophyte_Leaf_Cells.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Chlorophyll-a-3D-vdW.png/500px-Chlorophyll-a-3D-vdW.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Chlorophyll-a-3D-vdW.png/500px-Chlorophyll-a-3D-vdW.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Space-filling model of the chlorophyll a molecule","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChlorophyll-a-3D-vdW.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Chlorophyll_Extraktion.jpg/500px-Chlorophyll_Extraktion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Chlorophyll_Extraktion.jpg/1280px-Chlorophyll_Extraktion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Chlorophyll_Extraktion.jpg/500px-Chlorophyll_Extraktion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chlorophyll forms deep green solutions in or